(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-07"] = {"11.1":{"s":"Surface chemistry studies what happens at the surface where two phases meet, such as a solid and a gas or a solid and a liquid. Atoms at a surface feel unbalanced forces, so surfaces behave differently from the bulk. This gives rise to adsorption, catalysis and colloids, which are the three topics of this chapter and have many uses in industry and daily life."},"11.2":{"s":"Adsorption is the sticking of molecules of a gas or a liquid on the surface of a solid or a liquid. The substance that is adsorbed is the adsorbate, and the surface holding it is the adsorbent. Adsorption is a surface effect. Absorption is different: there the substance enters the whole body of the material, as water goes into a sponge. Both together are called sorption.","w":["Adsorption happens only on the surface; absorption goes through the whole volume. Water taken up by a sponge is absorption."]},"11.3":{"s":"Adsorption is of two kinds. In physical adsorption, called physisorption, weak van der Waals forces hold the gas on the surface. It is reversible, not specific, and can build up several layers. In chemical adsorption, called chemisorption, chemical bonds form between the gas and the surface. It is much stronger, specific to the pair of substances, and limited to one layer. Chemisorption is usually irreversible.","w":["Physisorption: weak, reversible, multilayer, falls as temperature rises. Chemisorption: strong, specific, one layer, often needs some heat to start."]},"11.4":{"s":"The amount of gas adsorbed depends on several factors. Gases that are easily liquefied are adsorbed more strongly. A larger surface area of the adsorbent, from fine powder or a porous solid, gives more adsorption. Adsorption gives out heat, so a rise in temperature usually lowers physical adsorption. Increasing the pressure of the gas increases the amount adsorbed, until the surface is full.","w":["Adsorption is exothermic: lower temperature favours physisorption.","More surface area means more adsorption: finely divided charcoal adsorbs more than a lump of the same mass."]},"11.5":{"s":"An adsorption isotherm shows how the amount adsorbed per unit mass of the adsorbent changes with pressure, at one fixed temperature. Freundlich proposed an equation that fits the adsorption of a gas on a solid, with two constants that depend on the system and the temperature. A graph of the logarithms is a straight line. At low pressure adsorption is nearly proportional to pressure, and at high pressure it levels off.","f":[{"x":"x/m = k*P^(1/n)","n":"Freundlich isotherm, n > 1. x = mass of gas adsorbed, m = mass of adsorbent, P = equilibrium pressure; k and n are constants. For a solution, use concentration C instead of P.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 11.5, p.163"},{"x":"log(x/m) = log k + (1/n)*log P","n":"Logarithmic form: a straight line of slope 1/n and intercept log k.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 11.5, p.163"}],"w":["The factor 1/n lies between 0 and 1. If 1/n = 1, adsorption is directly proportional to pressure; if 1/n tends to 0, it no longer depends on pressure."]},"11.6":{"s":"Adsorption has many practical uses. Activated charcoal in gas masks adsorbs poisonous gases. Silica gel removes moisture, and charcoal removes colour and smell in sugar refining and water treatment. Solid catalysts work by adsorbing the reactants on their surface. Adsorption is also used in chromatography to separate mixtures, and in creating high vacuum by adsorbing the last traces of gas."},"11.7":{"s":"A catalyst is a substance that changes the rate of a chemical reaction and is not used up in it. In homogeneous catalysis, the catalyst and the reactants are in the same phase. In heterogeneous catalysis they are in different phases, often a solid catalyst with gases. A catalyst does not change the final equilibrium or the heat of the reaction. A promoter increases the activity of a catalyst, and a poison reduces it.","w":["A catalyst speeds up both the forward and the reverse reaction equally, so it does not change the position of equilibrium.","A catalyst is chemically unchanged at the end of the reaction, though it takes part in the steps."]},"11.8":{"s":"The adsorption theory explains heterogeneous catalysis in five steps. The reactants diffuse towards the surface of the solid catalyst. They are adsorbed on it. An intermediate forms on the surface, and the product forms from it. The product is then desorbed and leaves the surface, so fresh reactant can take its place and the cycle repeats. This is why the catalyst stays unchanged in mass and in composition.","w":["Order for the exam: diffusion, adsorption, intermediate formation, product formation, desorption."]},"11.9":{"s":"A colloid sits between a solution and a suspension. Its particles are bigger than a molecule but too small to see, about 2 to 500 nm across; a solution has particles of 0.1 to 2 nm. The dispersed phase is spread through the dispersion medium. A colloid scatters a beam of light, the Tyndall effect, is not clear like a solution, and does not separate on standing.","w":["Particle size is the difference: a solution has 0.1 to 2 nm, a colloid 2 to 500 nm.","The Tyndall effect, the scattering of a light beam, is how a colloid is told from a true solution."]}};
